  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 3NT. West's opening lead drives out South's ♠A. There are only 5 top tricks (the ♠A, 2 hearts, the ♦A, and the ♣A). If West has the ♣K, South can take 4 additional club tricks by finessing in that suit.\n\nIt cannot hurt for South to cash the ♦A before playing on clubs. If East has a singleton ♦K, it will be possible to take 4 additional diamond tricks by finessing against West's ♦J and ♦9.\n\nWhen the ♦K drops, South continues diamonds, covering West's card. After crossing in hearts, South must cash the other heart honour, before finessing again in diamonds.\n\nIf East follows with a low diamond on the first round of that suit, South will have to depend on the club finesse.\n\nNotice that East could defeat this plan by dropping the ♦K on the above lie of the cards (among others)! Unless East is an expert player, however, South should take the play of the ♦K at face value and assume it is singleton.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠Q against 3NT. West's opening lead drives out South's ♠A. There are only 5 top tricks (the ♠A, 2 hearts, the ♦A, and the ♣A). If West has the ♣K, South can take 4 additional club tricks by finessing in that suit.",
    "It cannot hurt for South to cash the ♦A before playing on clubs. If East has a singleton ♦K, it will be possible to take 4 additional diamond tricks by finessing against West's ♦J and ♦9.",
    "When the ♦K drops, South continues diamonds, covering West's card. After crossing in hearts, South must cash the other heart honour, before finessing again in diamonds.",
    "If East follows with a low diamond on the first round of that suit, South will have to depend on the club finesse.",
    "Notice that East could defeat this plan by dropping the ♦K on the above lie of the cards (among others)! Unless East is an expert player, however, South should take the play of the ♦K at face value and assume it is singleton."
  ],
